.\" Title: vrunner-cert
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-cert.1

.TH VRUNNER\-CERT 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner\-cert \- manage named certificates for per-command access control

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBcert\fR \fBgenerate\fR \fINAME\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert\fR \fBlist\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert\fR \fBshow\fR \fINAME\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert\fR \fBremove\fR \fINAME\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
Manage named certificates in the vrunner certificate pool. The certificate pool provides per-command access control: each certificate has a derived bearer token, and commands can be bound to a specific certificate. API requests to certificate-bound commands must present the matching bearer token for authorization.

Certificates are self-signed X.509 certificates generated locally and stored in \fB~/.config/vrunner/certs/<name>/\fR. Each certificate has a derived bearer token computed as the SHA-256 hash of the certificate PEM content, hex-encoded as a 64-character string. The certificate pool is registered in the vrunner configuration file.

Unlike TLS server certificates (which encrypt the transport), certificate pool entries are for application-level authorization. They allow fine-grained access control in multi-tenant environments where different consumers need access to different commands.

.SH SUBCOMMANDS

.SS cert generate \fINAME\fR

Generate a new self-signed X.509 certificate and add it to the certificate pool. The certificate and private key are saved to \fB~/.config/vrunner/certs/<name>/\fR, and the pool entry is registered in the configuration file.

See \fBvrunner\-cert\-generate\fR(1) for details.

.SS cert list

List all certificates in the pool, showing name, certificate file path, key file path, and the first 16 characters of the derived bearer token.

See \fBvrunner\-cert\-list\fR(1) for details.

.SS cert show \fINAME\fR

Display full details of a specific certificate, including the complete 64-character bearer token. Use this token in the \fBAuthorization: Bearer\fR header when making API requests to certificate-bound commands.

See \fBvrunner\-cert\-show\fR(1) for details.

.SS cert remove \fINAME\fR

Remove a certificate from the pool registry. The PEM files on disk are \fBnot\fR deleted. The certificate can no longer be used to bind new commands.

See \fBvrunner\-cert\-remove\fR(1) for details.

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Generate a certificate for a CI pipeline:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner cert generate ci-pipeline\fR
Certificate 'ci-pipeline' generated successfully.
  Certificate: /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/ci-pipeline/cert.pem
  Key:        /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/ci-pipeline/key.pem
  Token:      a3f8c1e2b7d94001... (first 16 of 64 chars)
.fi
.RE

.PP
List all certificates:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner cert list\fR
NAME                     CERT FILE                                         TOKEN (prefix)
my-app                   /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/cert.pem   a3f8c1e2b7d94001
ci-pipeline              /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/ci-pipeline/cert.pem 7c1e9d4a8f2...
.fi
.RE

.PP
Show full token for API authentication:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner cert show my-app\fR
Certificate: my-app
  Certificate: /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/cert.pem
  Key:        /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/key.pem
  Token:      a3f8c1e2b7d94001b8c3d2e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6
  Token (16): a3f8c1e2b7d94001...
.fi
.RE

.PP
Remove a certificate:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner cert remove old-app\fR
Certificate 'old-app' removed from pool.
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Successful operation.

.TP
.B 1
Error (certificate not found, generation failure, etc.).

.SH SEE ALSO

.BR vrunner\-cert\-generate (1),
.BR vrunner\-cert\-list (1),
.BR vrunner\-cert\-show (1),
.BR vrunner\-cert\-remove (1),
.BR vrunner (1),
.BR vrunner\-spawn (1)
